 first Muslim Chaghatayid, and it is not unreasonable to suppose that his faith may have been one reason why the Chaghatayid noyad gravitated to other princes such as Algun and Baraq. Only slightly more than a decade had passed since the death of Chaghadai, with his well-known devotion to yasa and yosun and his antipathy towards Muslims. This is not to say that religion and tradition were the only factors; both Alghu and Baraq possessed military skills superior to those of the youthful Mubarak Shah. Despite this, Islam had some influence oven among the non-believers, as Baraq allegedly converted on his deathbed. While Islam surely found converts after Baraq among the Mongols, it wasn’t until the 1330s that it truly became a political factor. DeWeese suggests that during this period some used Islam as a method of gaining support in a rather contentious political environment.”  '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1523, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1439})
